{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1502001-the-new-minor-spheres-thread","id":1502001,"title":"The New Minor Spheres Thread","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2023-02-02T20:33:40-08:00","id":1502001,"title":"The New Minor Spheres Thread","content":"As many on here may know, I am something of a fan of the minor spheres. Some share my like of this particular style of magic in the game and others don't. What I am looking to do here is to create a list of peoples most beloved minor spheres that they have created. Throw in your notes complete or not but what I am trying to do here is to actually compile notes with the actual powers from dots one to five rather than just kick around ideas about what would be a good idea to do someday. So I'm not trying to shut anybody down from sharing their favourite pet idea, I'm just trying to learn other people's best fleshed out Minor Spheres<br /> <br /> I will contribute my own over the next few days. <br /> <br /> Lastly, I am aware of the new fan released supplement on the storytellers vault, the book of fragments, it has a number of minors spheres in it which apparently are quite good.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3148-zennis","name":"Zennis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3148_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"414"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-02-03T07:48:11-08:00","id":1502022,"title":"","content":"I would actually recommend <b>Book of Fragments</b> as a starting point for this: it doesn't just have nine rather good minor Spheres, but it also has some solid guidelines for making them (starting with something to the effect of \u201cthe above from <b>Revised Storyteller's Handbook</b> doesn't work very well; it doesn't give the Spheres enough meat)\u201d. <b>Book of Fragments</b> recommends that you select four Spheres and a concept, with the concept being kind of like a Sphere s Specialty except that it can apply to all four Spheres; and the idea is that each rank in the Minor Sphere in question lets you use any Effects that any of those base Spheres can accomplish individually or in conjunction with no more than that many dots, so long as it fits the concept; though in practice, it recommends that you provide two or three sample Effects for each level similar to the ones provided for each of the Major Spheres.<br /> <br /> The trick is to come up with a concept that's meaningfully limiting. That said, the concept need not be as limiting as it might first appear: <b>Book of Fragments</b>' Fire doesn't just deal with <i>literal </i>fire; it also deals with metaphorical fire. In fact, what <b>Promethean: the Created</b> calls \u201cPyros\u201d, the animating force that allows the Frankenstein's Monsters of that game to exist, is an example of the sort of thing that <b>Book of Fragments</b>' Minor Sphere of Fire could do. It also points out that the Children of Knowledge refer to it as <i>Multiplicatio</i>, the final stages of transformation.<br /> <br /> In that regard, I wouldn't mind seeing a similar treatment of other elemental Minor Spheres: for example, an Earth Sphere that isn't <i>just</i> about manipulating literal rocks and earth, but which also broadens the concept to more metaphorical applications such as \u201cfertile soil\u201d or stability and structure. Geomancy, too. part of the fun of coming up with Minor Spheres is coming up with some of the less obvious uses for them.<br /> <br /> <b>Book of Fragments</b> also recommends that you take steps to differentiate Minor Spheres from Pillars, with the main distinction being that a Minor Sphere is less tied to one specific approach to magick than a Pillar is. When designing Minor Spheres, try to think of different ways that the Sphere might be conceptualized by different Traditions or other factions, such as the aforementioned notion that the Solificati see Fire in terms of alchemical processes. <i>\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b</i><br /> <br /> \u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":421,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","user":"Dataweaver","date":". 03-07-2023, 12:03 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-02-06T10:04:28-08:00","id":1502311,"title":"","content":"I use several of what you could call &quot;Minor Spheres&quot;, but I use the Gurps system described on their book Thaumatology to build them. I play MtA using Gurps rules. With it, I can create an endless number of them.<br /> <br /> Examples are:<br /> - Pyrokinesis (sub-division of Forces but also a bit of Spirt, control of Fire and Heat but also relatable spiritual beings)<br /> - Cryokinesis (control of Ice and Cold and relatable spirits<br /> - Telekinesis (control of movement)<br /> - Electromagnetic Sphere (self explanatory)<br /> - Sound Sphere (self explanatory)<br /> - Illusion Sphere (mostly bits of Mind but also some Forces)<br /> - Empathy Sphere (Emotional manipulation, mostly sub-division of Mind)<br /> - Telepathy (Reading and controlling minds exclusively)<br /> - Necromancy (one particular kind of &quot;Sphere&quot; which I call &quot;Super-Spheres&quot;, that combines aspects of Life, Matter, Prime, Entropy and Spirit)<br /> - Alchemy (my second kind of &quot;Super-Sphere&quot; that is more a &quot;theme&quot; rather than a proper subject of understand of reality; it can combine elements from a good portion of all the 9 spheres, with a strong focus on the functionality of Prime, under the purview of breewing stuff on labs)<br /> - Elemental Sphere (my third &quot;Super-Sphere&quot;, the traditional &quot;Earth, Air, Fire, Water&quot; control, combining aspects of Matter and Spirit and even Prime and others related to those)<br /> <br /> Other than that, I use several divisions based on specific uses of the &quot;typical&quot; 9 Spheres. For example, &quot;Plant Sphere&quot; is a sub division of Life that deals exclusively with plants; &quot;Birds Sphere&quot; deals exclusively with plants and so. That kind of division can be as specific as desired (I dont know why would anyone ever want a &quot;Primates Sphere&quot; instead of a &quot;Mammal Sphere&quot; for example but hey, if you want to, go for it). Likewise, &quot;Metals Sphere&quot; or &quot;Liquids Sphere&quot; are also viable sub divisions on Matter, and &quot;Elementals&quot; is a viable sub-division of Spirit, or something like &quot;Fae Sphere&quot; (for interactions with the Dreaming and its denizens)<br /> <br /> Because I use Gurps rules and Gurps have Character Points for every single trait, all of those are well balanced in terms of XP costs, and it has created for me a superb rich game world, to the point that the &quot;default&quot; 9 Spheres are barely ever used. In fact, it's rare in my games to find a &quot;Master of Forces&quot; - instead, you find &quot;Allaston, the Fire Mage&quot;, or &quot;Shayan, the Winter Queen&quot; and some such, or instead of the typical Verbana witch, you deal with the &quot;Plant Druid&quot; or &quot;The Wolf King&quot; and somesuch.<br /> <br /> It greatly turns my Mages into specialists and far more unique and themed.<br /> <br /> Those above are a few of the examples I've made, but there are several others, and I incentive my players to make new ones.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24689-kakost","name":"Kakost","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"586"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}